RELEASE NOTES, c3p0-0.14.0
==========================

This release is another security-focused release addressing less
common but still dangerous attacks based on JNDI injection.

c3p0 (via its mchange-commons-java supporting libraries) supported a
clever but rarely used facility intended to allow objects that
implement javax.naming.Referenceable but are not serializable to be
serialized indirectly, via serialized References. Unfortunately, this
introduces a JNDI-injection path, if an attacker can get an
application to deserialize an object, indirected via a maliciously
crafted reference.

Although c3p0 now locks down pretty tightly what a maliciously crafted
reference can do (within the c3p0 codebase), it's better never to
encounter and try to dereference such a Reference.

c3p0 now implements a false-biased config param:

   com.mchange.v2.naming.allowIndirectSerializationViaReference

which disables the potentially dangerous functionality by default.

In the spirit of locking down tightly what a malicious Reference
can do, c3p0 now enforces a whitelist over what classes
JavaBeanObjectFactory can instantiate:

   com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClassWhitelist

By default, it includes only c3p0-implemented concrete Referenceable
classes, preventing References from provoking instantiation of
arbitrary classes, potentially doing arbitrary mischief.

This release also generates BeanInfo classes for its DataSource and
ConnectionPoolDataSource implementations to ensure that "connection"
and "pooledConnection" are not introspected as JavaBean properties.
Various libraries and tools assume JavaBeans property reads are safe,
but triggering a call to getConnection() / getPooledConnection() on
a DataSource referencing a maliciously crafted JDBC URL is a potential
attack vector.

c3p0 previously included some work towards enabling reproducible
builds, via support of the SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH. However, that support
was incomplete, rendering builds in practice not reproducible.

c3p0-0.14.0 remedies those loose ends. When built properly
(see "Reproducible builds" in README.md), the binary and source
jar files (but not the doc jar!) should be bit-for-bit reproducible.

New configuration properties in this release:
   com.mchange.v2.naming.allowIndirectSerializationViaReference
   com.mchange.v2.naming.referenceableJavaBeanClassWhitelist

Thanks to Qing Xu for sharing a demonstration SSRF attack
via an adversarially crafted reference embedded as an indirectly
serialized object. Both the default disabling of deserializing
such indirected objects and the whitelist's omission of the
HTTP-requesting class would prevent this attack under c3p0-0.14.0.
